/T (A MAC \(Media Access Control\) address is a 48-bit hardware address permanently assigned to a device's network interface card \(NIC\) by the manufacturer. It is written as six pairs of hexadecimal digits, for example 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E. The first half identifies the manufacturer \(OUI\) and the second half is a unique serial number. MAC addresses work at the Data Link layer and are used to deliver data to the correct device inside the same local network.)
/E (A MAC \(Media Access Control\) address is a 48-bit hardware address permanently assigned to a device's network interface card \(NIC\) by the manufacturer. It is written as six pairs of hexadecimal digits, for example 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E. The first half identifies the manufacturer \(OUI\) and the second half is a unique serial number. MAC addresses work at the Data Link layer and are used to deliver data to the correct device inside the same local network.)
