<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 112 0 R
/T (Data is never sent in one big piece. The TCP/IP protocols break it into small units called packets \(usually up to about 1500 bytes each\). Each packet carries a header \(source and destination IP address, sequence number, TTL\), a payload \(part of the data\) and error-checking information. Routers forward each packet hop by hop towards the destination; different packets may take different paths. At the destination, TCP checks for missing packets \(requesting a resend if needed\), puts them in the right order and rebuilds the original data.)
/E (Data is never sent in one big piece. The TCP/IP protocols break it into small units called packets \(usually up to about 1500 bytes each\). Each packet carries a header \(source and destination IP address, sequence number, TTL\), a payload \(part of the data\) and error-checking information. Routers forward each packet hop by hop towards the destination; different packets may take different paths. At the destination, TCP checks for missing packets \(requesting a resend if needed\), puts them in the right order and rebuilds the original data.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [32]
>>
